          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-09-22 06:43:43.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "Yonatan Nachum",
          "email": "ynachum@amazon.com",
          "date": "2026-10-05 14:53:55.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20791 efalnd: Make connection last use time atomic",
        "message": "LU-20791 efalnd: Make connection last use time atomic\n\nThe connection last use time is updated whenever a connection is looked\nup, and lookups only take a read lock on the NI connection lock, so\nseveral CPUs can update the same connection at once on every send and\nevery receive. The connection daemon also reads it while draining\nconnections without taking that lock at all.\n\nOn 64 bit a naturally aligned 64 bit access is indivisible, so a reader\nalways sees either the old or the new timestamp and never a mixture of\nthe two. The races are therefore harmless in practice.\n\nMake the field atomic, as the TX send time already is. Reading and\nsetting an atomic are relaxed operations that compile to a single load\nor store on 64 bit, so nothing gets slower.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Yonatan Nachum <ynachum@amazon.com>\nChange-Id: I2a6cf517a0216f8d713606ca53a782fb1b7f2550\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
  },
